"""Editable manuscript for the Horde Studio v18.3.5 illustrated manual."""
PAGES=[]
def add(ch,title,body,shot=None,table=None,example=None,terms=None,diagram=None):
 PAGES.append(dict(ch=ch,title=title,body=body.strip(),shot=shot,table=table,example=example,terms=terms or [],diagram=diagram))
add('Getting started','How to use this manual', '''This manual follows the Horde Studio v18.3.5 interface. Use the linked contents to open a task, the PDF bookmarks to move between sections, and the alphabetical index to find a setting. Search with Ctrl+F on Windows/Linux or Command+F on macOS. Screenshots are real application screens captured in an isolated demonstration library; Maya Chen and Harbour Museum Mystery are teaching examples, not included character packs.
For your first session, follow Installation, Connect a text model, and Choose your experience. Then take either the Virtual Human first conversation route or the World first playable scene route. You can finish both without generating any images or video.
Each topic explains where to go, what to enter, and what to check. The example boxes contain suggested authored text; they are not model responses. Screenshots show the relevant part of the interface and can be enlarged in your PDF reader. Advanced controls belong after the basic route works.
The app saves different kinds of data in different places. Character templates define starting content. Timelines contain played history. A text transcript is useful for reading but cannot restore a playable campaign. The backup chapters explain which export to choose.''',terms=['Search','Screenshots','Table of contents'])
add('Getting started','Installation and first launch','''1. Download the v18.3.5 portable ZIP from the official GitHub release. Extract the entire archive into a normal writable folder. Keep the app folder beside the launchers; do not move one launcher away from the rest of the package.
2. Install Python 3 if it is not available. The portable package includes the Node runtime for supported macOS, Windows and glibc Linux architectures. A source checkout, older package, or Alpine/musl system needs Node 18 or newer.
3. Run the launcher for your operating system. Leave its terminal running while using the app. Open the local address shown by the launcher; the default is http://127.0.0.1:43127. Opening index.html directly skips the local services.
4. Use a modern desktop browser. Keep the same browser profile and local address when returning to your library. Browser data belongs to that origin.
5. Open Settings, connect a text model, and test one small conversation before adding optional services. No npm install or application build step is needed for the portable package.''',table=[['System','Launcher'],['Windows','Start Horde Studio.bat'],['macOS','Start Horde Studio.command'],['Linux / Chromebook Linux','start-horde-studio.sh']],example='macOS / Linux terminal alternative:\nchmod +x start-horde-studio.sh\n./start-horde-studio.sh',terms=['Installation','Python','Node.js','Launchers','Local address'])
add('Getting started','Choose your experience','''The main sidebar switches between independent workspaces. Choose the mode that matches what you want to do; you do not need to configure every workspace before starting.
Chat Library is the fastest route to character roleplay. Worlds adds structured places, time and consequences to a setting. Virtual Humans 2.0 adds a persistent personal life around conversations. Video Adventures plans and renders a branching film. Multiplayer shares a Chat or World session with a party. Pip helps you find settings locally.
Personas describes you or the player character. Settings holds shared connections and application defaults. Customize changes presentation. Labs is optional local cognition. Pause agency is a convenient stop for proactive messages, social posts and background generation; direct replies can still work. Review the individual running life as well when troubleshooting activity.''',shot='home-library',table=[['Start here if you want...','Workspace'],['A character conversation','Chat Library'],['A setting with persistent game state','Worlds'],['A person with routines and relationships','Virtual Humans 2.0'],['A branching generated film','Video Adventures'],['A shared party session','Multiplayer']],terms=['Chat Library','Worlds','Virtual Humans','Video Adventures','Pause agency'])
add('Getting started','Connect a text model','''1. Open Settings > Connections. Configure the provider you intend to use. Cloud services need an account key; local or self-hosted services need their OpenAI-compatible base URL and, if required, a key.
2. Open Settings > AI & Models. Choose that provider, refresh its catalog, then select or type an exact text model ID. A provider name is not a model ID. The ID is sent as entered.
3. Save the settings. Check the connection using the available test and a short real conversation. A saved key or populated model field alone does not prove generation works.
4. When using a local model, start its server and load a model before testing. Enter its API base URL, including /v1 when that server requires it. Do not paste a webpage URL or append /chat/completions to a base that Horde extends itself.
5. Open the character or World model page to confirm the effective provider and model. Per-character and per-world choices can override the shared default. Virtual Human photos and voice use separate connections.
If the catalog is empty, verify the URL/key and model availability. An exact custom ID can work even when catalog discovery is unavailable.''',shot='settings-models',terms=['Connections','Text model','API key','Local models','Model catalog'])
add('Getting started','Connections, local servers and credentials','''Settings > Connections exposes the supported text accounts: OpenRouter, GPTProto, NanoGPT, NVIDIA NIM, Amazon Bedrock, Custom API and Local / self-hosted. Configure only the service you use. Account-specific endpoints and headers must match that provider's instructions.
Custom API uses an OpenAI-compatible contract. Horde calls BASE_URL/models and BASE_URL/chat/completions. Use HTTPS for cloud services or localhost HTTP for a local server. For a LAN text server, the application's Content Security Policy must allow that machine's address; changing an endpoint alone may not be enough.
Cloud generation sends the required prompt and references to the chosen service. Review the connection's remember/device-storage option before saving a key on a shared computer. Exports do not substitute for reconnecting provider accounts on another installation.
Use the URL your model server actually reports. Common local defaults are examples, not guarantees. A server must expose a compatible API, have a model loaded, and accept the configured model identifier.''',shot='settings-accounts',table=[['Local application','Common API base'],['LM Studio','http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1'],['Ollama','http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1'],['KoboldCpp','http://127.0.0.1:5001/v1'],['llama.cpp server','http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1']],terms=['Custom API','CSP','OpenRouter','GPTProto','NanoGPT','NVIDIA NIM','Amazon Bedrock'])
add('Getting started','Personas and player identity','''Open Personas in the sidebar to create the identity you want the character or campaign to encounter. Give it a name, an optional portrait, and a concise description of relevant background, voice and preferences. Keep facts concrete and avoid revealing a secret the character should not know.
Select the intended persona before starting a conversation or campaign. Session Setup in World Play also defines the played identity and story preferences. A World starting life can provide world-specific skills and perks; a general persona does not automatically grant every ability you describe in prose.
Virtual Humans share one persistent life across persona-specific conversations and relationships. Switching persona is not a new human or an automatic reset of their world. Use a separate life/timeline when you need a genuinely separate history.
In Multiplayer, each participant has a display name and public persona brief. Write a brief that is useful to the party; guests do not receive the host's private World data.''',example='Example player persona:\nAlex Reed - a visiting archives researcher. Friendly, observant, and unfamiliar with the town. Knows restoration terminology but has never met Maya.',terms=['Personas','Player identity','Session Setup'])
add('Character chat','Create or import a character','''Open Chat Library. Use New Character for an original card, or Import for a supported Horde/Nexus file or SillyTavern PNG card. Inspect imported instructions, greetings and provider settings before playing.
In Character Studio, start with Basics: name, description/personality, scenario and opening greeting. Write the greeting as the scene you want the player to encounter. Add example dialogue only when it demonstrates a useful voice or interaction pattern.
Open AI Config and confirm the effective text model. Use the shared default initially. Save the character, then begin a short chat. Refine one field at a time after observing the response.
The AI Builder is a drafting tool for character authoring. Give it a focused description, choose a generator model and review the resulting fields before saving. This is separate from the retired whole-human generator in Virtual Humans.
A card is a starting template. Its played conversations and persistent memory are separate session data. Sharing a card does not necessarily include all the histories you have played.''',shot='character-basics',example='Scenario: The player arrives at a coastal museum after closing. The curator needs help identifying a mislabeled painting.\nGreeting: "You must be the researcher. I hope you brought a notebook - our catalogue has become a puzzle."',terms=['Character cards','Import','Character Studio','Greetings','AI Builder'])
add('Character chat','Conversation tools and timelines','''Send a message with a clear action or question. Use Continue when you want the character to advance without a new player utterance. Reroll requests another answer and can incur another provider request. Inspect the conversation after a reroll so the story follows the version you intended.
Use the available message actions to edit or manage individual messages. Editing visible prose is not the same as correcting every memory or game-state value; inspect those separately if continuity has already changed.
Keep separate timelines for alternate stories. Continue an existing history when you want continuity; start fresh when you want a clean beginning; fork when you want an alternate branch while retaining a previous path. Name timelines descriptively, such as Museum - first meeting.
A downloaded transcript preserves readable text. Use application backups for restorable library data. Before deleting a timeline, export what you want to retain.
Optional image mode appears only for a character whose image capability, provider and model are configured. A successful image attachment must contain actual decodable image output, not just a written image prompt.''',terms=['Reroll','Continue','Timelines','Fork','Transcript','Image generation'])
add('Character chat','Presets, sampling and context','''Character Studio > AI Config controls the prompt preset and generation parameters. Begin with a modest configuration that your model supports. A larger context allows more history and instructions but can slow local generation or increase cloud usage.
Max Tokens limits generated output, while Context Size budgets the overall request. Temperature changes variation. Top P, Top K, Min P and penalties are provider/model-dependent; unsupported controls may not have the effect you expect. Change one parameter at a time and compare a repeated test scene.
The included Freaky Frankenstein 5.4 Internal States preset provides modular prompt, styled dialogue and state presentation. Fine-Tune lets you disable modules. A 32k context and roughly 4k output tokens are a practical starting point for its full configuration; reduce modules on smaller local models.
World Play uses only compatible preset modules, with canonical World state remaining authoritative. Virtual Humans do not use this character preset. Older saves retain their selected version until you explicitly change it.
Reasoning effort applies only where the selected model/provider supports it. More reasoning may add latency or token use; it is not a replacement for coherent authored facts.''',shot='character-ai',terms=['Presets','Freaky Frankenstein','Temperature','Context Size','Max Tokens','Reasoning'])
add('Character chat','Lorebooks, author notes and regex','''Character Studio > Lore Book holds background entries that can be injected on keywords or kept constant. Use short titles, relevant trigger words and concise facts. World Truth describes an established fact; Character Knowledge describes what the character believes. Keep mistaken beliefs distinct from actual truth.
Probability controls whether a triggered entry fires. For essential information, use a reliable trigger or a constant entry. Too many constant entries consume context even when unrelated to the current scene.
Author's Note adds a compact writing direction at a chosen injection depth and frequency. Use it for tone, pacing or an immediate instruction. Avoid repeating the entire character profile. Disable it when the direction is no longer needed.
Regex scripts and output cleanup can transform displayed/generated text. Review imported scripts and test with a short message. An over-broad rule can strip legitimate dialogue or required formatting. If output disappears or looks corrupted, disable the newest script and compare.
Settings > Behavior includes immersion and output-cleanup controls. Preset styling, author notes and regex can interact, so test them together before a long campaign.''',shot='character-lore',example='Lore entry: East Gallery\nKeywords: east gallery, missing painting\nWorld Truth: The empty frame belongs to a landscape on loan from the Reed family.\nAuthor note: Keep clues concrete; ask before advancing to the next location.',terms=['Lorebooks','World Truth','Character Knowledge','Author\'s Note','Regex'])
add('Character chat','Memory, HUD meters and group rooms','''Character Studio > Memory and the in-chat memory tools let you inspect long-term continuity. Pin important facts, edit mistakes and archive obsolete entries. Settings > Memory controls retrieval and consolidation. An embedding model and a consolidation model perform different jobs; use compatible models for each connection.
Memory is a selected record of evidence, not perfect recall of every token. Keep essential authored facts in the card/lorebook as well. If an old claim returns, inspect pinned and retrieved entries before repeatedly correcting the same chat reply.
HUD & Meters adds an optional status panel to ordinary chat. Define up to eight bounded meters, their meanings and starting values. Choose manual updates, the main chat model, or Labs local cognition. Each timeline retains its own values. A decorative meter is not a World rule unless that mode explicitly enforces it.
Group Rooms combine a cast under shared room settings. Create a room from the library's room view, add the participating characters and confirm the room's shared model. Keep names and voices distinct. Use the room's session controls for alternate histories.
Begin with two characters and one simple scene; expand only when turn order and identity remain clear.''',shot='character-hud',terms=['Memory','Embeddings','Consolidation','HUD','Meters','Group Rooms'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Understand the person and the life','''A Virtual Human has an authored character and an evolving life. Edit human defines identity, psychology, relationship starting context, expression and the starting world. Live human controls a particular persistent timeline: current location, routine, people, possessions, media, memory and events.
Start with identity and one working text connection. You can chat before starting a persistent life. You do not need supporting people, a job, a full week of appointments, Maps, photos or video to begin.
When a life exists, its state influences availability and conversation. A sleeping or occupied character may defer a reply intentionally. System health and character availability are different checks: a healthy service can show a character who is currently unavailable.
Different player personas maintain their own conversations and relationship histories inside the shared life. An imported full life is a separate restored copy. Editing the template does not automatically rewrite all prior lives.
Think of the template as the starting dossier and the timeline as what happened after that start. Read save receipts carefully to see which destination was updated.''',shot='vh-edit-overview',diagram='person-life',terms=['Edit human','Live human','Persistent life','Template','Availability'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','First conversation: the shortest setup','''1. Open Virtual Humans 2.0. Select the included Aslyn Jonas to explore a complete authored example, or choose New human to create your own.
2. Open Edit human > Identity & history. Enter a name, age, personality and a short background. Use a fictional adult example with a clear occupation or interest. Photos are optional.
3. Open Conversation & expression. Describe how they write and add one brief example exchange. Keep the voice natural and consistent.
4. Open Models & providers. Select the text provider whose credentials you configured in Settings, then choose a conversation model. Confirm the effective model, not just the catalog selection.
5. Choose Save human. Read the save status. Open Chat and send a small greeting such as "Hi Maya - how is the museum restoration going?"
6. Confirm that a real reply arrives before adding life simulation. If it fails, check the provider, exact model ID and reply details. Do not solve a connection failure by filling every optional character field.
This route creates a usable conversation without starting background life or submitting image/video work.''',shot='vh-chat-unstarted',example='Beginner dossier:\nMaya Chen, 29, museum conservator. Patient and observant, quietly funny. Recently moved to a coastal town. Writes short thoughtful messages and admits uncertainty. The player is a new acquaintance.',terms=['New human','Aslyn Jonas','First conversation','Save human'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Identity, appearance and history','''Edit human > Identity & history describes who the person is. Use name, pronouns and age consistently. Occupation and social world explain the pressures and opportunities that shape everyday conversation.
Write personality as concrete behavior. "Careful and skeptical; asks for evidence before agreeing" is easier to enact than a stack of abstract adjectives. Behavior examples can show how the same trait changes under stress, with strangers or among friends.
Background explains meaningful history. Avoid declaring that the human has already shared experiences with the player unless that is an intentional starting relationship. Appearance should describe stable visual traits; do not pack the whole personality into the photo prompt.
The interface also exposes appearance and body/access settings in the relevant identity/media controls. Review their help and boundaries deliberately rather than accepting generated assumptions.
Relevant fields have a ? help control and character counts. Oversized pasted text stays in the editor and blocks saving with the field name and limit. Shorten that field; do not assume it was silently clipped. Very long profiles may also exceed later provider context budgets even when the editor can save them.''',shot='vh-edit-identity',example='Personality: Maya notices small inconsistencies and asks one precise follow-up. She is warm with familiar people but needs time to trust strangers. Under stress, she becomes terse rather than theatrical.',terms=['Identity','Appearance','Backstory','Character limits','Field help'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Psychology and starting relationship','''Edit human > Psychology & relationship contains values, contradictions, vulnerabilities, habits, private life and relationship style. Give each field a different job. Values explain priorities; contradictions create tension; habits describe recurring choices.
Starting scenario and initial motive explain why contact makes sense now. Player knowledge defines what they actually know about the selected player. Avoid omniscient knowledge of the player's private thoughts or real-world identity.
Set prior contact and relationship context to match your story. For a first meeting, choose the never-spoken/new-acquaintance path and write only facts they could know. Existing friendship requires a concise authored history, not a claim that unrelated stored chats happened.
Relationship style and boundaries guide expression. Live relationship pace and current emotional state belong to the selected running life. A change in the template is not a promise of an immediate specific autonomous event.
Use fewer strong details rather than contradictory instructions. Test the voice through several situations before adding more psychology. Strange, solitary or fictional characters can have sparse social lives; a full modern lifestyle is optional.''',shot='vh-edit-mind',example='Values: Preserve objects and tell the truth about their provenance.\nContradiction: She dislikes status games but wants her work recognized.\nInitial motive: Find a reliable outside perspective on the museum catalogue.',terms=['Psychology','Relationship context','Prior contact','Boundaries','Player knowledge'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Conversation, expression and opening messages','''Edit human > Conversation & expression controls writing voice, conversational habits, example exchanges and habits to avoid. Describe vocabulary, punctuation, message length and cadence in terms you can observe.
Keep sample exchanges short. They demonstrate voice, not completed shared history. Avoid samples that force a romantic relationship or a specific reply regardless of the actual context.
Choose who starts the conversation. If the human starts, write the opening message; an empty opening in that mode blocks saving. Choose the player-starts option when you want to initiate naturally.
A specialized chat-style drafting tool can propose style fields and voice details for review. Check its output against the person before applying it. The character model remains separate from any model you choose for drafting.
Live emotions, attention and memory can change how the authored voice appears in a particular moment. If every reply sounds identical, reduce repetitive examples and avoid directing the model to repeat a signature phrase each time. If it becomes overly verbose, specify brief messages and test with a simple exchange.''',shot='vh-edit-chat-style',example='Player: Was the delivery useful?\nMaya: yes. one frame was mislabeled, though.\nPlayer: Another mystery?\nMaya: a very small one. my favourite kind.',terms=['Chat style','Conversation & expression','Opening message','Cadence'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Use AI drafts without losing control','''Use the AI draft action beside one field when you want a targeted suggestion. Page-level drafting starts with no fields selected. Select exactly the fields you want, enter your direction and review the displayed request count before generating.
Each selected field makes a separate small text request. The tool does not generate the whole human or their world in one response. Credentials, numeric controls and actual media generation remain explicit settings/actions.
Review the proposed text. Edit it, select only the results to keep, and choose Apply selected drafts to editor. Applying copies text into the editor; it does not finish saving. Choose Save changes and close or the main Save human / Save changes action.
Stop generating preserves finished results in the open review window. Retry only the failed field after resolving its error. Closing the review window discards results that have not been copied to the editor.
For life sections, use Review completed sections or Retry missing sections after interruption. Finished sections are retained while the dialog remains open. Read dependency warnings: a room must link to a place, and a routine needs an actual destination.''',example='A focused drafting request:\nDraft only Personality and Behavior examples. Preserve Maya\'s museum job and reserved voice. Show curiosity through precise questions. Do not invent a shared past with the player.',terms=['AI draft','Apply selected drafts','Request count','Retry missing sections'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Build a small starting world','''Open Edit human > World & autonomous life. The starting-world cards lead to Places & rooms, Wardrobe & presets, People, Routine & goals, Identity references and Models & providers.
For a first life, add one home, one regular destination and a small activity set. Keep place names stable. Add one supporting person only if they matter to the character. You can leave optional social and media systems empty.
Use Start manually to author details yourself, or Generate a section to request one focused draft. AI helpers show their section request count. Places, people, clothing and routine are separate choices; generation never requires a full cast and schedule.
Check schedule timing and run Audit VH before starting. Resolve overlaps or insufficient travel gaps. A valid character can still have intentionally sparse setup; an audit helps distinguish an empty optional system from a broken dependency.
The starting world is used when creating a persistent life. Once a life is running, edit its routine and places in Live human. Saving a changed template alone will not relocate the current life or rewrite its history.''',shot='vh-edit-life',example='Minimal world:\nHome: Harbour apartment.\nDestination: Town museum.\nOptional social place: Corner cafe.\nActivities: Read restoration notes; walk along the harbour.\nSupporting person: Jo Patel, museum coordinator.',terms=['Starting world','World & autonomous life','Audit VH','AI helpers'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Start the persistent life and verify it','''1. Save the authored person and confirm the text connection works in Chat.
2. Open Live human. Before a timeline starts, this page explains the difference between the saved character and persistent state. Choose Review starting world to inspect its setup.
3. Review routine, people, places and generation permissions. Choose Start persistent life when you are ready. Starting can use the configured text provider and enabled allowance.
4. In Overview, confirm that a persistent timeline exists and inspect its current activity, location and service health. Open Routine & goals and Places to confirm the authored setup arrived.
5. Return to Chat. The status shows the character's availability. Try one message when they have free attention. If no reply arrives, inspect the waiting reason before changing identity fields.
6. Pause the life when making major repairs or when you want to prevent further background activity. Resume after checking the saved changes.
The local launcher/service must remain running for local background simulation. Closing the computer does not leave a hidden local simulation running. Catch-up can resume when the service returns.''',shot='vh-start',terms=['Start persistent life','Overview','Pause','Resume','Catch-up'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Read the life overview and reply status','''Live human > Overview is the first place to inspect what is happening now. The current activity and place describe simulation state; system health describes whether the service and connections are working.
In Chat, Life status shows waiting reasons and controls such as Pause/Resume, activity editing and access to restart. A busy commitment can allow attention breaks; private time and sleep can defer a response. This is different from an HTTP error or missing model.
If a reply is delayed, check in order: Is the life paused? Is the character sleeping or in private time? Is a commitment occupying the day? Is the provider configured? Does Reply details show a failed request? Repair the specific cause instead of restarting immediately.
Use Text usage & reply attempts to inspect foreground and background requests. Provider-reported token usage appears when available; an unavailable report is not a zero-cost report.
The app no longer enforces daily text-request caps, including older saved shared caps. Provider billing, credits and rate limits still apply. Automatic posts and adviser reviews can make text requests while enabled.''',shot='vh-live-overview',terms=['Life status','Waiting reason','Text usage','Reply attempts','Daily text caps'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Routines: commitments versus activities','''Open Live human > Routine & goals. A recurring commitment reserves time, such as work from 09:00 to 17:00. An everyday activity is an option the person may choose during an opportunity window; its actual duration is a separate value.
1. Add commitment for a fixed obligation. Set its days, start/end, place and relevant availability. Leave free periods between obligations and enough time to travel.
2. Add activity for a flexible action. Define when it may be chosen, how long it lasts, and the place or conditions it requires. Do not set a meal to occupy the whole day when you mean a short opportunity.
3. Review Now and the schedule timing message. Use Fix schedule conflicts to inspect overlaps and travel gaps. Save through the relevant dialog/action and verify the displayed activity.
4. Add goals or plans only when they have a clear target and fit the calendar. Shared plans and trips can constrain the same clock.
For debugging, start with an empty schedule or one commitment. Add the next rule after seeing the first behave as expected. Routine expresses possibility and pressure; it does not guarantee the model will narrate a particular scene.''',shot='vh-live-life',example='Commitment: Museum shift, weekdays 09:00-17:00, Town museum.\nActivity: Read restoration notes, opportunity 18:00-21:00, duration 30 minutes, home.\nKeep a gap for the journey home and leave time for sleep.',terms=['Routine','Commitments','Activities','Opportunity window','Schedule conflicts'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Places, rooms and local time','''Open Live human > Places. Add place for a location such as home, museum or cafe. Open place & rooms to define interiors. A room is inside a place; it is not a second city-level location.
Give each place a stable name and type. Add meaningful details for activity and photo continuity. Configure the residence and local-time settings in the relevant advanced/world setup controls. Check geography before expecting live weather or realistic routes.
A fictional place can work without map coordinates. The interface may show Coordinates not set; this does not mean the character's identity failed. Use authored travel estimates or an offline world pack when real Maps is unnecessary.
For photo continuity, describe the room's distinguishing features and link approved references. A location can have geographic data without a dedicated visual reference; map discovery does not automatically produce room art.
To remove a place, first change any current-location, journey, routine, residence or active-rule dependencies. Remove place cannot erase a location still required by the life. Removal keeps recorded history and archives linked references. Do not delete the current home to fix a route error.''',shot='vh-live-places',example='Place: Harbour apartment (home).\nRoom: Work corner - a narrow oak desk, green task lamp and framed harbour map.\nRoom: Living room - navy sofa, pale walls, books on conservation.',terms=['Places','Rooms','Residence','Local time','Offline world pack','Remove place'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Maps, travel and transport','''Open Live human > Providers, feeds & events > Set up Maps when you want geographic grounding. Configure the chosen provider, then search a saved place and review the match. Do not accept the first similarly named venue without checking its address and coordinates.
Automatic live routes is a separate switch. Off uses authored travel estimates; manual Maps searches can still work. Turning it off stops future automatic submissions. The UI displays calls and local limits shared across lives for each provider.
Places and advanced transport controls hold saved routes and scheduled services. A route must connect actual saved places. Review journey timing, arrival location and commitments after changing it.
Air travel - no key needed estimates a simulated journey using open geography. Its label is not a confirmed commercial booking or live flight status. Optional flight-provider connections add their own setup requirements.
Trips, overnight visits and temporary stays affect the active life. Review travel disruption recovery when a journey or destination becomes invalid. Start with a home-to-work route before adding airports, recurring transport or long vacations.''',shot='vh-live-connections',terms=['Maps','Travel','Routes','Automatic live routes','Air travel','Transport'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Supporting people and independent lives','''Open Live human > People. Add person creates an authored supporting relationship. Define name, role, background, relationship and a meaningful location. A name in the social circle alone does not make that person an active simulated participant.
The page shows participation status. People with established residence and position are prepared automatically; explicit roommates start at the shared home. Others may show Needs location - not yet participating. Open person or Life & location and resolve that setup before expecting visits.
Use Prepare existing people after inspecting saved people and places. Draft people's routines with AI can propose independent schedules, but you must review destinations and timing. Keep their rules consistent with their occupation and the main human's world.
Meeting new people and Social connections govern opportunities and progression. Visits, contact and romantic development depend on the life and permissions; a prose friendship description does not force every encounter.
For a beginner, use one person with a clear home/work location. Check that they participate, then add a routine. Large casts multiply relationships, geography and scheduling dependencies.''',shot='vh-live-people',example='Jo Patel: museum coordinator, established colleague. Lives in town; works at Town museum. Friendly and practical, but does not know the player. Add a location before drafting Jo\'s weekday routine.',terms=['People','Supporting cast','Participation','Prepare existing people','Visits'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Relationships, emotions and memory','''Live human > Inspector exposes author-only needs, decisions, emotional state and remembered evidence. Internal state is not automatically player knowledge. A character can privately hold an impression without having stated it in Chat.
Inspect connection, emotions & history when a relationship or reply seems inconsistent. Check the selected persona and life first. A different persona has a different conversational relationship within the same shared life.
Relationship pace and personal boundaries control progression; experience and memory connect events to continuity. Use concrete authored expectations and inspect evidence instead of treating a single numerical value as a complete explanation of the person.
Conversation emotions affect expression alongside the template's voice. If a reply feels abrupt, inspect current activity, attention and recent evidence before changing the entire personality.
Inspector is useful for debugging and authoring, but avoid feeding private state back as if the player had learned it naturally. In a story, let disclosed information come through actual conversation and observed events. Export a full life before major recovery changes; VH2 timeline rewinds are not currently supported through this workspace.''',shot='vh-live-inspector',terms=['Inspector','Emotions','Relationship pace','Experience','Virtual Human memory','Timeline rewind'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Wardrobe, outfits and possessions','''Live human > Belongings expands to Wardrobe, Gifts & possessions and Money. Wardrobe separates style preferences, saved outfits and owned clothing. A style profile describes how combinations should look; an outfit is a particular combination; a garment is an individual item.
Start with a small practical closet. Give items stable descriptions and use references when visual consistency matters. Inspect current clothing after changing a preset or outfit; a written style intention is not the same as current worn state.
Gifts & possessions can include food, tickets, vehicles and other belongings. Review the life-owned permissions before sending gifts. Direct gifts, mailed gifts and money have visible keyboard-operable checkboxes and must be saved to the selected life.
Possessions can support activities and photo context. Keep related place/person references valid. Removing a room or reference should not leave an important object described in an impossible location.
Simulation possessions and money are fictional. They do not purchase a real item or pay a media provider. Provider generation charges remain governed by separate connections and media permissions.''',shot='vh-live-closet',example='Starter wardrobe:\nNavy cardigan; cream linen shirt; dark trousers; practical trainers.\nMuseum outfit: shirt + cardigan + trousers.\nHarbour walk: cardigan + trainers, with a light raincoat if appropriate.',terms=['Wardrobe','Outfits','Garments','Gifts','Possessions'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Money, costs and autonomy permissions','''Live human > Money shows fictional balances, purchases, currencies and recurring expenses. Use it when finances matter to the authored life. Keep income and costs plausible for that setting rather than adding a bank balance with no supporting routine.
Purchases and wardrobe conversion can connect acquired items to possessions. Inspect the saved result and any receipts after a change. Financial state belongs to this timeline; changing the character template does not retroactively refund a purchase.
Autonomy is a set of permissions and configured opportunities, not a single promise that every feature runs. Review exploration, spontaneous plans, check-ins, background posts, adviser reviews and media rendering individually.
Begin with automatic images/posts off, verify ordinary conversation and the routine, then enable one background behavior at a time. Inspect its activity before enabling the next. Pause agency in the main sidebar helps stop proactive work quickly; use life controls for the selected timeline as well.
Horde does not enforce a daily text-request cap in this version. Media spending controls and provider-side limits are separate. A fictional money balance does not limit your real provider invoice.''',shot='vh-live-money',terms=['Money','Recurring expenses','Autonomy','Adviser','Background posts','Spending'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Photos and identity references','''Edit human > Appearance, photos & voice configures the image capability, provider/model, personal photo direction and identity anchors. Start by selecting a supported image connection. A working text model alone cannot generate photos.
Use one clear identity reference that matches the authored appearance. Additional approved angles can help supported models. Describe stable features in appearance and use photo direction for framing, camera style and exclusions.
Test one manual photo before enabling autonomous images. Confirm actual image bytes appear in the app. A written prompt or failed download is not a completed photo. Review Image activity for submitted work and errors.
Live human > Reference Library organizes portraits, character sheets, room and place references, clothes and props. Approve relevant references and link them to the correct entity. Provider reference count and format limits still apply; more images are not always better.
For a new character, keep identity, one room and one outfit visually consistent before building a large reference bible. Existing imported starter assets do not prove that your newly configured provider can generate fresh images.''',shot='vh-edit-voice',example='Photo direction: Natural window light, documentary framing, normal proportions. Preserve dark shoulder-length hair and brown eyes. At home, use the navy cardigan and the green task lamp in the work corner.',terms=['Photos','Identity reference','Photo direction','Image activity','Reference limits'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Reference Library and asset bible','''Open Live human > Reference Library. References are grouped so a portrait, room, place, garment or prop can be found and reused. The library is a visual continuity system, not the life clock or a replacement for authored facts.
1. Select the entity you want to illustrate. Check that its name and description match the life.
2. Add or generate a relevant reference through the available controls. Inspect the result before approving it.
3. Link it to the correct person, place, room or object. A beautiful room image linked to the wrong place can mislead later photo generation.
4. Test one scene using a small compatible reference set. Review the actual provider output for identity and setting continuity.
Aslyn's bundled example includes approved references for 22 authored places and six rooms. A newly discovered map venue may only have geographic data. It still needs its own authored/approved visual reference when you want a consistent interior.
Keep a concise art/asset bible: stable appearance, clothing, room features and recurring props. Export the human after verifying that referenced media is present. Export warnings about missing assets deserve inspection before deleting the original installation.''',shot='vh-live-references',terms=['Reference Library','Asset bible','Approved references','Visual continuity'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Social feed, gallery and media jobs','''Edit human > Social presence defines caption voice, posting rules and access boundaries. The social drawer in Chat contains Feed, Gallery and Clips. These belong to the shared life, even when conversations are persona-specific.
Feed shows published social content. Gallery can hold private photos and ideas. An idea can store a prompt and references without submitting a paid image. Treat a draft idea, a submitted job and a published post as three different stages.
Live human > Media is the author view for captured moments, generation jobs and publishing controls. Use Image activity to inspect successful output or failures. Automatic background photos have separate enable/limit controls from a manual generation action.
Review a caption and access setting before publishing within the simulation. A generation prompt is production direction; it is not automatically the text other characters see.
Photos retain their proportions. Use a clear identity reference and a relevant room/place reference, then inspect one manual result. If jobs fail, fix the provider or reference problem before queuing multiple retries. A provider timeout can leave an uncertain job; check its existing status first.''',shot='vh-live-media',terms=['Feed','Gallery','Media','Publishing','Background photos','Social presence'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Video clips and voice','''Edit human > Video & clips configures clip style and provider routing. The social drawer's Clips tab presents available clips. Generate a clip on demand rather than assuming every social moment automatically produces a video.
Write production direction describing subject, setting, movement, framing and continuity. Keep it separate from the public caption. Use Edit caption to change what viewers read below the video without treating that caption as the render instructions.
Supported references and model capabilities vary. Choose a provider/model that supports the type of clip you need, inspect the configured reference set and test a short example. Existing bundled clips are starter assets, not evidence of a new successful request.
Photos & Voice also configures voice notes/calls and browser or provider TTS options. Local speech servers are configured in Settings > Local Runtime when they implement the compatible speech endpoint. Choose the desired engine per human.
Calls share life transcript and attention. A sleeping or occupied human may not be available just because a voice engine is configured. Test text and life status first, then test speech playback. Check browser audio permissions if a successful speech response cannot be heard.''',shot='vh-edit-video',example='Clip production direction: Maya stands beside the museum workbench, turns a small frame toward the window and examines a corner. Slow handheld medium shot. Preserve the cardigan and workshop references.\nCaption: A small repair before opening.',terms=['Clips','Video','Edit caption','Voice','Calls','TTS'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','World information, feeds and events','''Live human > Providers, feeds & events separates Conversation, Photos, Places & travel, and World information. Configure each service only when you need it. Saving a provider key is not the same as verifying a response.
Add RSS / source URL for a feed you want the human to discover. Use the preview/validation flow and inspect recent titles. Find relevant feeds can propose sources, but review their relevance and validity before adding them.
Find Ticketmaster events is for configured event discovery, not automatic attendance or ticket purchase. An event must fit geography, time, interests and permissions before becoming a plausible plan.
Maps and live routes have their own quotas. Weather/daylight grounding depends on appropriate geography and enabled configuration. A fictional life can operate without live feeds or real venues.
Outside information becomes context through the configured awareness system; it does not give the character unlimited knowledge or make a social post a verified report. Check source labels and timings when an event looks stale or out of place.
Turn off optional automatic services you do not want running. Adding a source should be followed by one inspection of its actual items, not immediate broad background enablement.''',shot='vh-live-connections',terms=['RSS','Ticketmaster','World information','Weather','Feeds'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Save destinations, errors and diagnostics','''When editing a running human, the save destination can be Character template only or Template + this life's expression. Expression updates cover the person's expressive profile; live routines, places and references are edited through Live human.
Read the receipt after saving. The template can save successfully while the active-life update still needs attention. If the life update is not confirmed, reconnect and inspect the current life instead of assuming both destinations changed.
If device storage fails, the editor keeps the draft. Do not reload immediately or close the tab before preserving important authored text. Oversized fields must be shortened before saving; the error identifies the field and limit.
Edit human > More actions > Error log shows recent authoring errors for the current page session. Reloading clears that log. Saved provider-job failures remain available in Chat reply details and Image activity.
Life status > Text usage & reply attempts > Reply details exposes the attempt's model, status, reason, provider-reported token usage when available and a bounded redacted prompt preview. Record those details when reporting a failure. Avoid sharing keys or private conversations in a public report.''',terms=['Save destination','Save receipt','Error log','Reply details','Diagnostics'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Back up, share and restore a human','''Use Export to choose the kind of package you need. Clean character template is for sharing the authored person without personal player history. Full Portable Human includes conversations, saved life and media. Editable JSON Template is for hand-editing authored fields in a text editor.
New portable exports are .horde_human.zip files. Import the ZIP directly without unpacking. character.json is the manifest; media and compressed life backups are separate files. Older JSON .horde_human imports remain supported.
A restored life is an isolated copy; existing lives are not overwritten. Uncertain paid jobs are not automatically resubmitted. After importing, inspect the character, timeline, conversations, gallery/references and required provider settings.
Keep the original installation until the restored copy has been verified. Export reports missing assets and backup errors; do not ignore them and then delete the source.
Editable JSON keeps embedded starter media. Preserve embedded image/video data when changing text fields, then import through the ordinary Import button to create a separate character. Use Full Portable Human for historical backup rather than a text-edited template.''',table=[['Goal','Export choice'],['Share a fresh character','Clean character template'],['Preserve the full played life','Full Portable Human'],['Edit authored fields externally','Editable JSON Template']],terms=['Full Portable Human','Clean template','Editable JSON Template','.horde_human.zip','Restore'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Always-on server and recovery','''By default, the local server must remain running for background life. Catch-up resumes after downtime; no local process runs while the computer is off. A cloud text provider by itself does not keep the simulation alive.
Open Live human > Always-on private server to use the guided move to a private VH2 service in a hosting account you control. The flow keeps a paused local recovery mirror. The remote host needs persistent storage and a cloud-capable text connection; a local LM Studio endpoint cannot answer after this computer turns off.
Before moving, export a Full Portable Human and verify it. Follow the in-app host checks and service URL/authentication steps. Confirm the remote service is healthy and the intended life is active there. Keep the recovery mirror paused so two copies do not independently diverge.
Use the guided switch-back and recovery controls rather than copying arbitrary database files over a running service. Inspect which instance owns the active life after recovery.
The repository's deploy/vh2-self-host/README.md covers complete hosting, persistent storage and recovery details. This is advanced optional setup; a new user can remain local until ordinary conversation and the life work reliably.''',shot='vh-live-recovery',diagram='hosting',terms=['Always-on private server','Recovery mirror','Hosting','Catch-up','Persistent storage'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Worked example: Maya\'s first complete life','''Build this example in stages and check each result before moving on. It is deliberately small enough to diagnose.
1. Author Maya's identity and short texting voice. Use a new-acquaintance player persona. Save and obtain one successful text reply.
2. Add Harbour apartment and Town museum. Add one room to the apartment. Check that every room links to its intended place.
3. Add a weekday museum commitment and one evening reading activity. Leave travel/free-time gaps. Run Audit VH and resolve schedule/dependency errors.
4. Add Jo with an actual location. Prepare existing people and check that Jo participates. Add Jo's routine only after the location is valid.
5. Review permissions with automatic media/posts off. Start the persistent life. Inspect Overview, Routine & goals and Places. Test Chat when Maya has attention.
6. Add one identity reference and one room reference. Test a manual photo, review Image activity, then consider an optional background image allowance.
7. Export a Full Portable Human. Import into a separate copy and inspect the timeline and media before treating the backup as verified.
If a stage fails, repair that stage. Do not expand the cast or buy more provider credits to compensate for an invalid route, sleep rule or model ID.''',example='Success checklist: a saved person; a working text reply; valid home/destination; a sensible routine; an active supporting person; visible life state; optional tested media; a verified portable backup.',terms=['Maya Chen','Worked example','Virtual Human setup'])
add('Worlds','Understand a World template and campaign','''A World template defines the setting: locations, people, items, factions, lore, starting lives, rules and presentation. A played timeline contains the player's location, clock, inventory, stats, quests, NPC state, history and consequences.
Edit the template in World Studio. Play in World Play. New starting quests and Starting Lives apply to new timelines; changing them does not silently rewrite an existing campaign. Use Timeline > Correct world state when you deliberately need to repair current canonical state.
The narrator describes the scene, while Horde's local rules and state systems govern movement, checks, inventory and other enabled mechanics. A confident sentence in the prose is not proof that a canonical item or quest changed. Inspect the HUD and records after important actions.
Start with a small map and one playable situation. Add rules and society systems only when the setting needs them. Off / Pure Narrative disables optional mechanics while retaining core movement, world canon, memory and NPC identity.
The included worlds provide different examples: Vaelora for a medieval sandbox; Bellwether for a large town; The Shattered Crown for a campaign; Policy Panic for workplace comedy.''',shot='home-worlds',diagram='world-flow',terms=['World template','Campaign','Canonical state','Starting Lives','Policy Panic'])
add('Worlds','First playable scene: build a small World','''1. Open Worlds > Create New World. In Basics, name it Harbour Museum Mystery and write a concise description, narrator/global rules and an optional exact intro.
2. Open Locations. Replace the blank starter location with Museum lobby; add East gallery and Harbour cafe. Select the intended starting location in its location settings. Connect lobby and gallery with valid exits.
3. Open People. Add Jo, the museum coordinator, at the lobby. Define identity and a short knowledge entry. Keep a hidden motive separate from public description.
4. Open AI Config. Leave the model blank to use Settings, or intentionally pin a model. Confirm that a short text request can work.
5. In HUD, Stats & Quests, choose Off / Pure Narrative for the first test. Set a sensible start clock and minutes per turn. Optional stats/quests can be added later.
6. Save World, run World Audit and resolve broken references. Choose Save & Enter World. Complete Session Setup for your player.
7. Inspect the start location, send one action, and use a visible exit to move. Check the HUD after each step. Export the template and a playable campaign once the test succeeds.''',shot='world-overview',terms=['Create New World','Save & Enter World','World Audit','World setup'])
add('Worlds','Basics: narrator, rules and opening','''World Studio > Basics defines the World's name, description, banner, Dungeon Master Persona / Global Rules and optional Intro Message.
The short description helps you find the world in the library. The narrator field describes voice, genre, point of view and constraints. Use concise operational directions such as "Do not reveal hidden motives before evidence is discovered" rather than a long list of contradictory stylistic demands.
Intro Message is the exact narration shown when a new session begins. Leave it empty if you want the AI to improvise an opening. An intro should place the player coherently at the actual starting location and introduce an actionable situation.
A banner is optional. Supported uploaded images are normalized for the card/play background. Visual art does not create locations or rules; author those in their own pages.
Save the World before entering. Re-enter a new timeline when testing a changed opening or Starting Life. An existing campaign has its own played history and should not be used as a clean-template test.''',shot='world-basics',example='Narrator: Grounded coastal mystery; concise scenes and concrete clues. Respect canonical location and inventory. Keep Jo\'s private knowledge hidden until discovered.\nIntro: Rain taps the lobby windows. Jo points toward the east gallery. "The frame is here. The painting isn\'t."',terms=['Basics','DM Persona','Intro Message','Banner'])
add('Worlds','Locations, regions and connected exits','''World Studio > Locations shows compact location cards and regions. Use + Location for an actual playable place, and + Region to organize larger maps. Open a location card to edit its full simulation and visual record.
Name locations clearly and choose an appropriate type: room/interior, building, street/path, yard/outdoor, area or transit hub. Use a parent/region relationship to express containment, and explicit exits to express movement. Being in the same region does not create a route by itself.
For each exit, choose an existing destination, travel time and any condition or one-way rule. If players should return, author the reverse connection or use the relevant bidirectional control. Check that names and target references remain consistent after renaming.
Cross-region travel links actual departure and arrival locations. You can author transport such as walking, train or starship, along with route name, minutes, cost and one-way behavior. A decorative map line or prose mention is not enough.
Use World Architect to inspect the graph visually. Run World Audit after deleting or moving locations. Test every essential exit in a fresh timeline, including locked/unlocked routes and return journeys.''',shot='world-locations',example='Museum lobby <-> East gallery: walk, 1 minute.\nMuseum lobby <-> Harbour cafe: walk, 8 minutes.\nEast gallery is inside the museum; the cafe is a separate building.',terms=['Locations','Regions','Exits','Cross-region travel','World Architect','One-way routes'])
add('Worlds','People, knowledge and NPC schedules','''World Studio > People defines persistent NPCs. Add a stable identity, personality, appearance, current/default place and relevant knowledge. A public description introduces the person; private facts and beliefs belong in the appropriate knowledge/authoring fields.
Use authored relationships and affiliations when they matter. Avoid making every NPC know all hidden World facts. Distinguish what Jo saw from what Jo suspects. NPC identity remains active even when optional RPG modules are off.
NPC Schedules assigns recurring activity/location rules. Confirm that referenced places exist and the schedule makes sense with travel. Enable NPC Schedules in the applicable World controls when you want schedule-driven movement.
In play, inspect the present cast and state after time advances. A person may leave because their schedule changed, not because the narrator forgot them. Test a short time interval and a crossing of a scheduled boundary.
For large worlds, develop one coherent region at a time. A hundred shallow NPC names are harder to debug than a small cast with valid locations, knowledge and relationships.''',shot='world-entities',example='Jo Patel - museum coordinator. Public: efficient, raincoat still damp. Knowledge: the gallery was intact at 08:30; the delivery driver arrived at 08:45. Private uncertainty: Jo has not checked the loading entrance.',terms=['NPCs','People','NPC Schedules','Knowledge','Affiliations'])
add('Worlds','Items, equipment and commerce','''World Studio > Items creates authored item cards. Use them when possession, equipment, bonuses, requirements or trade needs a structured rule. Give each item a stable ID, readable name and clear description.
HUD, Stats & Quests controls Inventory, Equipment & item bonuses, Money & commerce and equipment slots. A tool can add a check, stat or skill bonus while equipped; an item in the inventory is not necessarily equipped.
Choose a currency stat/name for commerce. Keep health, money and XP out of ability checks unless you deliberately intend them as check stats. Starting Lives can provide possessions; new item definitions do not automatically grant them to every existing player.
During play, verify acquisitions, consumption, equipment and purchases in the HUD or inventory. Do not rely only on narration that says "you now have the key." Check the canonical result and any relevant receipt.
Legacy text inventories still work, but item cards are the right place for real bonuses and requirements. Start with one key and one useful tool, test pickup/use, then add shops, prices and complex equipment.''',shot='world-items',example='Conservation kit: practical tools for examining a frame. Add a small examination bonus only if the appropriate equipment/check modules are enabled.\nGallery key: opens the staff entrance; define the actual access condition separately.',terms=['Items','Equipment','Inventory','Commerce','Currency','Item bonuses'])
add('Worlds','Factions, lore and private information','''World Studio > Factions describes organizations and power groups. Define their purpose, relationships and relevant places. Use factions when affiliations or society consequences matter, not simply as another text block to repeat the narrator.
Global Lore holds setting knowledge. Separate established truth from a person's belief and from information the player has discovered. A hidden faction goal should remain hidden until the story provides evidence.
Author's Note is a compact direction for narration, with depth/frequency controls where provided. Use it for current tone or pacing. Avoid instructions that contradict the actual clock, location, inventory or quest state.
Living society and political systems can connect factions to territory, legitimacy, conflict and obligations. Start with authored foundations before enabling procedural growth. A generated development should fit the existing map and powers.
Test with a player who does not know the secrets. Ask an ordinary question and confirm the narrator does not disclose every private record. In multiplayer, only the sanitized visible transcript and permitted room metadata are shared with guests.''',shot='world-factions',terms=['Factions','Global Lore','Private information','Hidden state','Author\'s Note'])
add('Worlds','Starting Lives and sandbox society','''World Studio > Sandbox defines Starting Lives and optional society simulation. A Starting Life can change the opening place, role, title, possessions, stats, faction, reputation, duties and opening scene.
Create one starting role first, save the World, then start a New timeline to test it. An existing timeline keeps the life it began with. An obligation is story context; add a tracked starting quest separately if it needs progress and rewards.
Society switches cover settlement food/wealth/safety/unrest, politics and succession, conflict/war, law/reputation, seasons/harvest and procedural growth. Choose an appropriate scale and calendar, then write principles that explain what can change.
Enable only the systems relevant to your setting. A small museum mystery may need no kingdom politics. A medieval sandbox needs settlements, powers, geography and plausible seasonal pressure before procedural developments become useful.
Inspect changed state after time advances. Keep a clean template export and a tested campaign backup before making large society-rule changes. Optional modules can be changed without deleting their saved data, but their absence changes what is enforced during play.''',shot='world-sandbox',example='Starting Life: Visiting researcher. Start at Museum lobby, modest funds, a notebook and examination skill. Obligation: help catalogue the loan collection. Add a separate tracked quest if that obligation needs rewards.',terms=['Sandbox','Starting Life','Living society','Politics','Seasons','Law','Procedural growth'])
add('Worlds','Choose mechanical depth and HUD modules','''Open HUD, Stats & Quests. Choose a rule pack or customize the modules individually. Off / Pure Narrative is a good first test; Light adds selected everyday mechanics; Full RPG enables a broader rules set. Mystery and Adventure offer other starting configurations.
Player stats, health/defeat, conditions, checks, inventory, equipment, commerce, quests, relationship scores, NPC schedules and off-screen simulation are optional modules. Core movement, memory, world canon and NPC identity stay active.
HUD visibility is separate from rule enforcement. Hiding a module does not mean its rules are disabled. Likewise, an attractive displayed value is not automatically an ability-check modifier.
Define custom stats with stable internal IDs, display names, initial values, min/max and color. A maximum of 0 means uncapped. Mark only appropriate abilities Usable in checks, and choose a supported roll formula/direction.
Choose the vital/health stat and fail-forward or lethal defeat behavior deliberately. Lethal mode can produce a timeline game over. You can pause/restore optional mechanics from World Play > More > Mechanics without deleting saved character data.''',shot='world-hud',terms=['Mechanical depth','Rule packs','Off','Light','Full RPG','HUD modules','Health','Defeat'])
add('Worlds','Dice checks, skills, perks and flaws','''HUD, Stats & Quests > Check engine controls resolution mode, base die, stat modifier, difficulty, roll visibility and critical minimum/maximum behavior.
Automatic resolution lets the engine roll. Player-roll mode lets the DM request a canonical check and stop before the result; the Roll button resolves that exact check, persists it across rerolls, then asks the DM to narrate the authoritative outcome. Rerolling prose is not a new chance to roll the same check.
Per-stat mechanics lets each usable stat define its formula. Older direct/ability modes remain for compatibility; do not assume a money value of 100 means a +100 roll bonus. Resource stats usually should not be usable in checks.
World-specific Skills, Perks and Flaws belong to this World, not the reusable persona. Enter one record per line in the displayed format and set the starting point budget. Progression is optional.
Test one easy and one hard check before a long campaign. Inspect the die, modifier, difficulty and outcome. Explain requirements through canonical rules and item/skill configuration rather than hoping the narrator enforces a paragraph consistently.''',example='Capability format:\nExamination | Inspect objects and notice restoration details | 1 | 2\nLocal contact | Knows museum staff | 2 | 1\nOutsider | Unfamiliar with town customs | 1 | -1',terms=['Dice','Roll','Difficulty','Skills','Perks','Flaws','Check engine'])
add('Worlds','Quests, consequences and the clock','''Starting Quests are objectives granted to each new timeline. Add a clear goal and define the supported progress/reward conditions. Existing timelines are not changed by editing the template's starting list. Rewards occur on completion, not when the quest is created.
The clock has starting hour/minute, minutes per turn and a weekday/day-count configuration. Travel and other actions can affect time. Check the actual HUD after an action rather than assuming every utterance advances exactly one identical interval.
Persistent consequences record injuries, social fallout, legal trouble, losses and discoveries when enabled. Configure maximum active records and escalation/decay defaults deliberately. A severe unresolved outcome can worsen; minor outcomes can fade according to the configured rules.
Avoid a tracked quest with no achievable objective or an impossible dependency. For a first World, use one clue-discovery goal and test its completion in a fresh timeline.
NPC schedules and off-screen developments share the evolving world. After a long wait or journey, inspect people, place, quests and consequences together. If something is wrong, correct the specific current state and preserve a backup; do not try to fix every system through narration alone.''',example='Starting quest: Inspect the empty frame. Make the required discovery achievable in East gallery.\nConsequence example: Trespassing reported - a recorded legal/social outcome, with a clear resolution rather than indefinite vague suspicion.',terms=['Quests','Rewards','Consequences','Clock','Minutes per turn','Escalation','Decay'])
add('Worlds','AI Config, scene compiler and repair policy','''World Studio > AI Config selects the DM model and scene-compiler options. Leave the model blank to follow Settings; choosing a model intentionally pins this World.
The one-call scene compiler sends a compact authoritative scene packet instead of the whole world. Its location budget prioritizes the current, adjacent and explicitly referenced places. Local mechanics still resolve locally.
Memory retrieval can use the canonical ledger without embedding calls, or semantic archives with extra background calls. Choose the canonical route for a simple first setup. Semantic memory needs a working compatible embedding configuration.
Missing receipt policy controls uncertain canon changes. Adaptive repairs likely changes; Always repair can add requests; Never repair freezes uncertain state. A narrative success may therefore be unconfirmed state under the selected policy. Inspect records after important actions.
The optional Background World Agent runs after responses when future momentum is low. It proposes grounded off-screen developments and cannot move, harm or reward your player. The local kernel can advance known schedules/consequences without an API request.
Use a model that follows the structured tool contract. If text looks good but state changes fail, inspect the receipt/repair policy and model behavior before enabling more agents.''',shot='world-ai',terms=['Scene compiler','Missing receipt policy','Background World Agent','Canonical ledger','Semantic archives'])
add('Worlds','World Architect, Architect Agent and AI Builder','''World Architect is the visual map editor. Use it to inspect locations and connections. Keep graph links consistent with actual exits; map decoration does not override travel rules.
Architect Agent expands/refines a World conversationally. Describe the desired change, choose scope, change policy, detail and batch size, then Plan changes. The agent shows assumptions, batches and expected calls before generation. It stages typed changes for review rather than rewriting live state invisibly.
Use Add only for safe expansion, Fill gaps when completing sparse records, or the available safe-edit policy when deliberate edits are needed. Review changed locations, people, lore and dependencies before applying/saving.
AI Builder is a separate authoring route for generated World content. Choose its generator model and a bounded description. Inspect resulting records, map links, starting locations and rules before entering.
Use small batches, such as adding a cafe and its owner, then run World Audit and test the result. Large generated settings are harder to verify. Keep a template backup before a major architect operation, and test a new timeline when opening content changed.''',shot='world-architect-agent',example='Architect request: Add a harbour cafe connected to Museum lobby by an eight-minute walk. Add one owner with a grounded routine. Preserve existing IDs, clues and starting location. Stage changes for review.',terms=['World Architect','Architect Agent','AI Builder','Staged changes','Change policy'])
add('Worlds','Visual presentation and consistent art','''World Studio > Visuals enables optional presentation. Existing/imported worlds remain Classic until enabled. Recommended Experience can choose Classic Reader or Cinematic; Allow players to switch view preserves a player choice.
Set art style and an Art Bible / Prompt Appendix for consistency. Define era, palette, materials, costume and camera language. Upload or generate location art, portraits and a map skin through the relevant media controls.
A map skin is decorative beneath the live map. It cannot add an exit or change a route. Background darkness and narrative panel opacity control readability; inspect a scene with both bright and dark art.
Choose the image provider/model separately from the DM text model. Refresh the compatible catalog or enter an exact supported ID. Do not treat an image-model recommendation in the interface as a guarantee of price or availability.
Uploaded/generated images are normalized, deduplicated and embedded in World exports/full backups. Inspect export warnings and restored assets. Switch back to Classic when performance, contrast or accessibility suffers; the simulation state does not depend on the cinematic skin.''',shot='world-visuals',example='Art bible: Coastal museum in overcast daylight, muted navy and warm wood, documentary realism, practical clothing. Keep gallery architecture consistent. No decorative text labels in generated room art.',terms=['Visuals','Cinematic','Classic Reader','Art bible','Map skin','Image provider'])
add('Worlds','Play: movement, actions and the status panel','''Enter World from the library and complete Session Setup. Confirm your persona/Starting Life, initial location and story preferences before the first action.
Use visible exits or the map for a known movement route. Write ordinary actions clearly: "I examine the frame's lower edge" or "I ask Jo when the delivery arrived." Avoid combining an entire day of travel, purchases and combat into one first test.
The status panel holds current location, clock and enabled game records. Read it after important actions. A scene can change in prose while a state mutation remains unconfirmed; inspect inventory, quest progress and any diagnostics before continuing as if the change happened.
Continue asks the DM to advance without new player input. Roll resolves a requested check when using player-roll mode. Reroll replaces narration but preserves canonical resolved checks. More exposes metadata, presentation, mechanics, summarization, memory and transcript tools.
On smaller screens, open the HUD/status controls when the side panel is collapsed. Prefer Classic view for a readable first session. Use Exit to return to the library without deleting the campaign.''',shot='world-play',terms=['World Play','Movement','Status panel','Continue','Roll','Metadata'])
add('Worlds','Map, ledger, memory and state inspection','''Map shows the locations and routes relevant to play. A reachable route must exist in authored/canonical state; asking the narrator to teleport does not create a legitimate exit. Check locked routes and travel requirements in the relevant records.
The persistent ledger summarizes World continuity. Inventory, quests, stats, NPC state and consequence records are structured alongside readable narration. Use the status tabs and inspection tools to distinguish a remembered statement from an enforced rule.
More > Memory opens World vector memories when configured. More > Summarize reduces readable history/context through the selected model. A summary is useful context, not a substitute for the playable save or a transcript archive.
Inspect state after a surprising outcome before correcting it. Identify the specific location, item, stat, quest or NPC fact that is wrong. World Audit checks authored consistency; a current timeline correction addresses played state.
When reporting a bug, include the timeline, action, expected change, actual HUD/record result and model/provider. A readable transcript alone can omit the structured record that explains the failure.''',shot='world-map',terms=['Map','Persistent ledger','World memory','Summarize','State inspection'])
add('Worlds','Timelines, corrections and audit workflow','''World Play > Timeline offers New timeline, Rename timeline, Correct world state and Delete timeline. Use named timelines for separate campaigns or tests. A new timeline starts from the World's authored setup and selected Starting Life.
Use Correct world state for an intentional repair to the active campaign. Inspect the proposed/current values and change only the inaccurate record. Editing narration alone does not necessarily update inventory, quests or NPC state.
World Studio > World Audit checks authored references and consistency. Run it after structural edits, generated content, location deletion, new schedules and rule changes. Fix the source dependency instead of adding prose that contradicts the map.
Use this iteration: save a template backup, make one change, audit, start a new test timeline, play the relevant action, inspect state, then retain or revise the change. Existing long campaigns should not be your only test of a new opening.
Deletion is permanent for the selected timeline unless you have a verified backup. Export a playable campaign before removing valuable history or making broad corrections. Read the export receipt and test an imported copy.''',terms=['New timeline','Correct world state','World Audit','Delete timeline','Rename timeline'])
add('Worlds','Export a template or a playable campaign','''World Studio offers Export world template and Export playable campaign. These serve different purposes. A template shares authored starting content and media; it does not include played timelines.
Export playable campaign creates a verified portable ZIP containing this World, every played timeline and its media. Use it to preserve the actual campaign state. Import the supported package through Import World, then inspect the restored timeline, map, HUD and assets.
World Play > More > Transcript downloads readable text. It is useful for reading or sharing a session, but cannot restore a playable campaign. Keep a campaign export alongside the transcript when the history matters.
A full application backup can preserve broader browser-library data. Virtual Humans also have service-owned life data, so use the human's Full Portable Human export for a complete individual life backup.
Keep the original until an imported copy is verified. Check export errors or missing assets before moving computers. Reconnect provider credentials as needed; a portable project is not a guarantee that another machine has your provider access.''',table=[['Need','Choose'],['Share a reusable setting','Export world template'],['Preserve played state and all timelines','Export playable campaign'],['Read the story as text','Transcript'],['Back up the broader library','Settings > Data & Backup']],terms=['.horde_world','Export world template','Export playable campaign','Campaign ZIP'])
add('Worlds','Worked example: Harbour Museum Mystery','''Build a three-location mystery with one NPC before adding complex RPG rules.
1. Set the narrator and intro in Basics. Create Museum lobby as the start, East gallery as the clue location and Harbour cafe as a later conversation location.
2. Connect lobby/gallery in both directions with one-minute travel. Connect lobby/cafe with eight-minute walks. Test returning to the lobby.
3. Place Jo at the lobby with knowledge of the morning delivery. Add a notebook and conservation kit only if inventory is part of this version of the mystery.
4. Begin with Off / Pure Narrative. Add a starting quest only when you want a tracked objective. Make its completion condition achievable and test that the reward is not granted at setup.
5. Enter a fresh timeline as visiting researcher Alex. Ask Jo about the delivery, move to the gallery, examine the frame, and return. Inspect location and clock after each movement.
6. Add Light/check mechanics only after the basic route works. Configure Examination as a capability and test one canonical check. Add visual art last.
7. Run World Audit and export both the reusable template and the played campaign. This gives you a small reliable starting project to expand.''',example='First three actions:\n"I ask Jo what happened before opening."\nUse the East gallery exit.\n"I examine the back and lower edge of the empty frame."\nCheck the saved location and any quest/check result before continuing.',terms=['Harbour Museum Mystery','World worked example','Starting location'])
add('Multiplayer','Host a shared Chat or World session','''Multiplayer is host-authoritative, turn-based shared-party play for Chat Library and Worlds. The host owns the canonical save and model connection. Virtual Human multiplayer is not included.
1. Start Horde with its launcher and open Multiplayer, or choose Play Together in Chat Library/Worlds.
2. Select Same Wi-Fi / LAN or Internet room, then select a character, group room or World. Use a tested single-player setup first.
3. Create the room and share its generated LAN link or HS1 Internet invite code with your intended players.
4. Each participant sets a display name/public persona brief. Players submit one action in a fixed order. After everyone is ready, the host explicitly commits the round.
5. The host runs the existing engine with the named actions. Only sanitized visible transcript, location and round metadata are published to guests.
The mode supports up to 12 players. World participants share one canonical party location rather than independent map positions. The host supplies the provider connection; guests do not receive its API key or hidden World state.''',shot='home-multiplayer',diagram='multiplayer',terms=['Multiplayer','Host','Guests','Play Together','LAN','HS1 invite','Commit round'])
add('Multiplayer','Invites, voting and Internet relay','''LAN rooms exist in the running Horde process. Keep the host launcher and browser active. Guests must be able to reach the LAN link; localhost refers to their own machine and is not a shareable host address.
Internet rooms require a configured online room server. Horde does not ship a centrally operated public relay. The Multiplayer screen includes setup guidance for the self-hostable Cloudflare Durable Object relay in multiplayer-relay/. Deploy it in your account, then enter its HTTPS address under Internet room > Online room server address.
Internet rooms last up to 24 hours. Credentials recover from session storage after a tab refresh in the same session and are deliberately excluded from exports. There is no host migration/account identity system.
Rerolls and hard timeline resets require majority votes. Approval does not mutate the save until the host applies the decision. Guests can submit their own turns and vote; the host alone commits rounds, applies approved decisions and closes rooms.
The public party service is separate from the localhost provider bridge. Do not expose the provider bridge to make a room work. Diagnose invite reachability, room status and turn order before changing API settings.''',terms=['Internet room','Online room server','Cloudflare relay','Voting','Host migration','Room lifetime'])
add('Video Adventures','Plan a branching film','''Video Adventures is a separate mode from simulation Worlds. It plans a persistent branching film: a text Director prepares choices and only the chosen path incurs video generation.
Open Video Adventures > Create Video Adventure. Define the premise, tone, recurring cast and references. Keep a first story small: one place, one character and two meaningful next choices. Confirm the Director text model before rendering.
In production settings, choose the provider route, primary renderer and optional fallbacks. Configure required keys through the relevant connection controls. Standard uses Fal; alternate adult-content routes use HotAPI where configured. Mandatory provider/model checks can still reject requests.
Choose resolution, duration, aspect ratio and session spending limit. Review the estimated block cost shown by the app rather than treating a printed manual as a current price list.
Save and open a timeline. Plan & prepare produces the next planned block. Review direction/choices and render the path you want. Keep production text concrete: subject, action, setting, framing and continuity. This mode does not inherit all the canonical map/RPG behavior of World Play.''',shot='video-studio',terms=['Video Adventures','Director','Fal','HotAPI','Plan & prepare','Spending limit'])
add('Video Adventures','References, render jobs and timelines','''Character reference handling offers Smart auto, direct supported multi-reference routes and a composed identity-anchor strategy. Smart auto selects a suitable route; an anchor may require a separate image-generation step. Reference support depends on the selected model.
Use consistent cast descriptions and a small approved reference set. Review the first rendered shot for face, costume, setting and motion continuity before extending the story. A successful planning response is not a completed video.
The play screen shows the player, timeline, choices and recorded spend. New timeline creates another story run; rename runs to distinguish branches. Choose a next beat before generating the corresponding video block.
Fallback renderers can improve recovery but may change style or incur another provider attempt. For an uncertain timeout, inspect the existing job/status before submitting a duplicate. Completed clips are downloaded locally through the bridge.
Short durations and modest resolution help with a first test. Provider model names, capabilities and prices change; use the live catalog and current estimates. Export/back up your broader app data and inspect what the supported backup includes before deleting a run or moving machines.''',terms=['Reference strategy','Identity anchor','Render jobs','Fallback renderer','Video timelines'])
add('Settings and local tools','Images: ComfyUI and compatible local endpoints','''Settings > Images configures MCP media and ComfyUI/local image servers. A local text server and local image server are different connections.
1. Start ComfyUI and confirm its actual URL, commonly http://127.0.0.1:8188. Export a working workflow in API format, not the ordinary visual-editor format.
2. Paste the JSON into the ComfyUI workflow field. Confirm or override the detected prompt and seed node IDs.
3. Supply a LoadImage node ID when the workflow should receive a Virtual Human identity reference. The graph must actually use that input for useful continuity.
4. Run the built-in photo test. Check that the bridge uploads references, queues the graph, polls history and retrieves a real image.
5. Select that image route per character/human and test manual generation before enabling background photos.
Private-LAN local image endpoints are supported where configured. The remote ComfyUI machine must listen on its LAN interface and allow the relevant port; public Internet hosts are blocked in this local-server flow. Keep uploaded reference data consistent with the workflow's expected formats.''',shot='settings-images',terms=['ComfyUI','API-format workflow','LoadImage','Local image server'])
add('Settings and local tools','MCP media, speech and certificate problems','''Settings > Images contains Higgsfield and Magnific MCP connection cards. Connect/authenticate through the local bridge, then select the provider per human under Photos & Voice. A connected bridge and a connected media account are separate checks.
OAuth credentials are stored outside the browser application in an owner-only mcp-auth.json file in the platform's Horde Studio configuration directory. Ordinary human exports and full browser backups do not include that file; reconnect on another machine.
For HTTPS certificate failures, the bridge uses its default trust store plus installed certifi or an explicit HORDE_CA_BUNDLE. Do not disable certificate verification. An expired provider/proxy certificate must be renewed by its operator. The repository's MCP_SETUP.md has the detailed procedure.
Settings > Local Runtime also exposes local speech integration for servers implementing POST /v1/audio/speech. Choose the local engine for each human in Photos & Voice. Test audio playback and browser permissions separately from text replies.
Keep the application launcher running; it owns the loopback bridge. If an MCP card says offline, inspect the launcher status and local address before repeatedly reconnecting the external account.''',shot='settings-runtime',terms=['MCP','Higgsfield','Magnific','OAuth','Certificates','HORDE_CA_BUNDLE','Local Runtime'])
add('Settings and local tools','Labs and TinyBrain local cognition','''Labs is optional support from a tiny local model. Open Labs in the sidebar to connect a compatible local runtime and select a model. The panel's diagnostics and guide help identify whether the runtime/model is ready.
Your main text, image and voice providers remain separate. Labs can support selected cognition/status functions where explicitly enabled; it is not a universal replacement for the conversation model or every simulation engine.
In ordinary character chat, HUD & Meters can use Labs as its update controller. Enable a single supported feature and test its output before enabling more local cognition. Local compute still consumes machine resources even when no cloud fee is involved.
If TinyBrain is unavailable, ordinary configured text chat can still work. Check the local server address, loaded model and capability requirements through the Labs diagnostics. Do not change the cloud API key to repair a local runtime that is stopped.
The application labels Labs Off when disabled. Preserve a simple working baseline, then compare behavior with Labs on. Choose the mode deliberately for the use case rather than enabling it because a setting exists.''',terms=['Labs','TinyBrain','Local cognition','HUD controller'])
add('Settings and local tools','Pip, customization and reading comfort','''Pip is the built-in local Horde Studio guide. Open Pip, ask where a feature lives, paste a redacted error or use the suggested prompts. It answers from the local handbook. TinyBrain may route a question, but Pip cannot alter a save.
Use Pip for product navigation and initial troubleshooting, then inspect the relevant feature's own status and diagnostics. A support answer is not a receipt proving a World correction or life change was applied.
Customize and Settings > Appearance adjust presentation, including editor text/window styling where provided. Choose readable font size, contrast and backgrounds. Test long text fields after changing editor appearance.
On smaller screens, side panels may collapse or group navigation. Open the relevant HUD/life group rather than assuming the feature disappeared. Use search inside Live human to find a setting by name. Expand advanced settings only when needed.
Classic World presentation is useful for accessibility and performance. In screenshots, zoom your PDF reader to inspect small UI labels; the manual's prose repeats the important path and action so the screenshot is not the sole source of instructions.''',shot='home-pip',terms=['Pip','Customize','Appearance','Accessibility','Workspace search'])
add('Maintenance and troubleshooting','Data storage, backup and upgrades','''Browser application data lives in IndexedDB under the Horde Studio origin. Changing the port, browser profile or site-data settings can make a library appear empty. Return to the original origin/profile before assuming data was deleted.
Virtual Humans 2.0 also stores persistent life state in the service's vh2-worlds.sqlite database in the application configuration directory. A browser transcript is not a complete backup of that service state.
Use Settings > Data & Backup for broader application exports, Export playable campaign for World histories, and Full Portable Human for individual lives. Verify a restored copy before deleting the original or replacing an installation.
To upgrade, export important data, extract the complete new portable package, restart the launcher and refresh the browser. Keep the same intended origin/profile and preserve the configuration directory. Do not overwrite random database files while the service is running.
MCP authentication and private-host tokens are deliberately omitted from ordinary exports. Reconnect required credentials after moving. A verified content backup and working provider access are two separate parts of recovery.
Keep one recent local backup and another copy outside the installation folder for important projects.''',shot='settings-data',terms=['IndexedDB','SQLite','Backup','Upgrade','Browser profile','Origin'])
add('Maintenance and troubleshooting','Service storage and database maintenance','''A long-running VH2 service can accumulate engine history and provider-job data. Open its storage controls to inspect the displayed policy and use Optimize service storage only when needed.
Before optimizing, pause every life on that service, wait for provider jobs to finish, close other connected Horde windows and ensure the host has enough free disk for a SQLite rebuild. The operation is service-wide, not limited to the currently selected human.
The optimizer creates verified checkpoints, applies the memory-retention policy, compacts completed-job payloads/old capture context, rebuilds the database and verifies integrity/canonical replay. It preserves exact chats, relationships, authored facts, media, important/pinned memories, active jobs and recovery snapshots.
Removed low-level diagnostic steps, stale routine/notable memory rows and completed-job payloads cannot be inspected afterward. Export important lives first. Existing photos are not recompressed by this maintenance action.
An upgrade can prevent future unbounded growth without instantly shrinking a large existing SQLite file. Unused database pages may remain reusable until reclamation or explicit rebuilding returns space to disk. Keep the app/server running until optimization verification completes.''',terms=['Optimize service storage','Database maintenance','Memory retention','Checkpoint','Free disk'])
add('Maintenance and troubleshooting','Troubleshoot by symptom','''Start with the narrowest visible failure. Check the launcher, selected origin, provider and effective model before changing a large profile. Inspect a saved receipt/job status before retrying uncertain paid work.
For Virtual Humans, distinguish a healthy but unavailable person from a failed service. For Worlds, distinguish incorrect prose from incorrect canonical state. For media, distinguish an idea, a submitted job and a retrieved asset.
Preserve a backup and change one setting at a time. Record the result of a short repeatable test. If the original symptom returns, compare the same scene, model and timeline rather than a new unrelated scenario.''',table=[['Symptom','First useful check'],['Library seems empty','Same browser profile and localhost origin/port?'],['No text reply','Effective model, credentials and request status'],['Human waits all day','Sleep/private time and commitment duration'],['Supporting person inactive','Residence/position and participation status'],['World cannot reach a place','Explicit exit target, lock and route conditions'],['Narration grants an absent item','Inventory/receipt and repair policy'],['Photo is only text','Actual image output and Image activity failure'],['MCP bridge offline','Launcher running and correct local address'],['Local model cannot be reached','Server started, model loaded, API base correct'],['Restored data lacks media','Export warnings and original asset availability']],terms=['Troubleshooting','No reply','Empty library','Missing media'])
add('Maintenance and troubleshooting','Provider errors and useful bug reports','''HTTP 401 commonly indicates an authentication problem; 403 indicates access/permissions; 402 indicates billing/credit trouble where used by the provider; 429 indicates rate/allowance limits. Inspect the provider's returned message and your account before retrying.
An empty catalog can mean model discovery failed, not necessarily that a typed exact model ID cannot run. A timeout can leave an uncertain provider job; inspect its existing status before resubmitting.
For VH text, collect Life status > Text usage & reply attempts > Reply details. For images, collect Image activity. For authoring, use More actions > Error log before reloading. For Worlds, include the action plus the saved HUD/record state and any receipt/repair message.
A good report names v18.3.5, operating system, browser, provider/model, workspace, exact steps, expected result and actual result. Attach a small redacted export or screenshot only when it helps reproduce the problem. Remove keys, host tokens and private conversations.
Use the repository's GitHub issues or official community links from the app. Keep the original failing backup until a fix is verified. Do not erase the evidence by resetting all settings as the first troubleshooting step.''',example='Report template:\nVersion / OS / browser:\nWorkspace and model:\nSteps to reproduce:\nExpected result:\nActual saved state or error:\nRelevant redacted diagnostics:\nDoes a new minimal example reproduce it?',terms=['HTTP errors','Rate limits','Bug report','Timeout','Redacted diagnostics'])
add('Reference','Feature map and quickest paths','''Use this map when you know the task but not its location. The displayed wording can shorten on narrow screens; the primary Edit human / Live human distinction remains the same.
For advanced life features, open the relevant navigation group or use Find a setting or feature. For template edits, save through the editor and read the destination receipt. For live changes, inspect the selected timeline after saving.''',table=[['Task','Path'],['Shared text credentials','Settings > Connections'],['Default text model','Settings > AI & Models'],['Image workflows / MCP','Settings > Images'],['Library backup','Settings > Data & Backup'],['Player identity','Personas'],['Character card / preset / lore','Chat Library > Character Studio'],['World opening / places / rules','Worlds > Edit > World Studio'],['Campaign repair','World Play > Timeline > Correct world state'],['Human identity and starting world','Edit human'],['Running routine / places / people','Live human'],['Human media jobs / references','Live human > Images & media'],['Human feeds / Maps / events','Live human > Settings & cloud'],['Reply failures and token reports','Chat > Life status > Text usage & reply attempts'],['Private hosting / recovery','Live human > Always-on server'],['Shared party','Multiplayer / Play Together'],['Local help','Pip']],terms=['Feature map','Settings paths'])
add('Reference','Glossary: authoring and simulation','''Template: authored starting content. It is different from a played timeline.
Timeline / life: a persistent instance with its own history and state.
Canonical state: the application's authoritative record, such as player location, inventory or a human's current activity.
Persona: the player's reusable identity. It is separate from the character/human being played with.
Commitment: reserved recurring time in a human's schedule.
Activity: a flexible opportunity with a separate duration.
Place / room: a saved location and an interior belonging to that location.
Supporting person: a known person in a human's life; participation also needs valid location setup.
Starting Life: a World-specific starting role and state for a new timeline.
Ledger: persistent continuity records used by the World system.
Receipt: confirmation/diagnostic evidence about a requested change or operation.
Consequence: a persistent outcome that can escalate, decay or resolve.
Inspector: author-only internal evidence, not automatically player knowledge.
Recovery mirror: a paused local copy retained when a life runs on a private server.
Catch-up: processing resumed after downtime; it does not mean a local computer simulated while powered off.''',terms=['Glossary','Template','Canonical state','Commitment','Activity','Recovery mirror'])
add('Reference','Glossary: models, media and storage','''Provider: the service or local server that receives generation requests.
Model ID: the exact identifier sent to a provider; a friendly provider name is not a model ID.
Context: the instructions/history/data available to a model in a request.
Max Tokens: the configured output limit; it is separate from context size.
Embedding: a vector representation used for semantic retrieval.
Consolidation: a process that summarizes/archives continuity for later use.
Reference: an approved visual asset associated with a person, room, place, garment or prop.
Asset bible: the organized visual continuity description/reference library.
Generation job: a submitted provider task; it may still be pending, failed or uncertain.
Caption: public text associated with media, distinct from production direction.
API-format workflow: executable ComfyUI graph data, different from ordinary editor layout JSON.
MCP: a connected tool protocol reached through Horde's local bridge.
IndexedDB: browser-origin storage for the application library.
SQLite: service database storage used by persistent VH2 lives.
Origin: scheme, host and port identifying browser storage, such as http://127.0.0.1:43127.
Relay: a multiplayer room service that coordinates actions and sanitized shared state.''',terms=['Glossary','Model ID','Embedding','Generation job','Caption','Origin','Relay'])
add('Reference','Sources, version and further reading','''This manual is based on Horde Studio v18.3.5, its current application interface, README, built-in help, Virtual Humans 2.0 Start Here guide, multiplayer guide, MCP setup instructions and private-server deployment documentation. Screenshots were captured from the application on 10 October 2026 using an isolated demonstration library.
Illustrations explain workflows; they are not screenshots. Authored example text is labeled as an example. No paid text/image/video output is presented as a successful live provider test. The included Aslyn character has starter content; this manual uses a neutral Maya example for setup screens.
Provider catalogs, prices, quotas and external setup requirements can change. Use the live application's catalog/estimates and your provider's documentation for account-specific details. The stable interface and data distinctions documented here apply to this release.
Official links below open the project and bundled guides. Consult the in-app help for individual field meanings and the deployment guide before administering a private host.''',table=[['Resource','Link'],['Stable release','https://github.com/ddkhan24/hordestudio/releases/tag/v18.3.5'],['Repository and README','https://github.com/ddkhan24/hordestudio'],['VH2 Start Here','https://github.com/ddkhan24/hordestudio/blob/v18.3.5/docs/vh2/START-HERE.md'],['Multiplayer guide','https://github.com/ddkhan24/hordestudio/blob/v18.3.5/docs/multiplayer.md'],['MCP setup','https://github.com/ddkhan24/hordestudio/blob/v18.3.5/MCP_SETUP.md'],['Private server deployment','https://github.com/ddkhan24/hordestudio/blob/v18.3.5/deploy/vh2-self-host/README.md']],terms=['Sources','Release','Further reading'])
# Additional hands-on topics are inserted into their related chapters below.
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Hands-on: add a museum work commitment','''Use this form after creating Town museum in Places. Open Live human > Routine & goals > Add commitment. The form uses the character's local time and affects future choices, not past events.
Enter Museum shift in Name. Select Monday through Friday. Set From to 09:00 and Until to 17:00, carefully checking AM/PM where the browser uses a twelve-hour clock. Read the duration statement: this should occupy 480 minutes, or eight hours.
Set Conversation availability to Busy - occasional phone checks if Maya can answer during breaks. Available to chat permits attention; Private - no phone checks defers it; Asleep - wait until awake represents sleep. Choose Town museum in Place rather than accepting the default home.
Choose Save commitment. Confirm the commitment appears in the routine and run the schedule check. Leave time for the journey and evening needs. Add a separate short Everyday activity for reading; do not reserve the full 18:00-21:00 window as three hours of mandatory reading.
If Maya stops replying all day, return to this form and inspect time, duration and availability before restarting the life.''',shot='vh-commitment-dialog',terms=['Add commitment','Conversation availability','AM/PM','Museum shift'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Chat media permissions and camera behavior','''Edit human > Appearance, photos & voice includes explicit chat media permissions. Allow generated photos and selfies in chat and Allow voice notes in chat control whether those capabilities appear among the human's tools. Disable a capability you do not want requested during conversation.
Neural voice audio is generated when a note is played. Background image rendering has its own enable/allowance controls in Live human; manual generation and chat permission do not automatically define the same spending boundary.
Who holds the camera? offers Smart context, Self-captured only, Allow plausible photographers and Follow scene wording. Smart context chooses an appropriate front-camera, mirror, timer, group or third-person approach from the actual scene and present people. Choose self-captured only for a deliberately personal phone-photo style.
Photo continuity minutes keeps follow-up context within the configured interval; 0 disables it. Use personal photo direction for consistent framing and appearance for stable physical traits. Do not force a photographer into an empty room through a caption.
Review provider/model and references, test one manual image, then inspect Image activity. A request timeout should be checked before another submission. Fictional gift/money permissions remain separate from these real media-generation actions.''',table=[['Control','What it governs'],['Chat photo permission','Photo/selfie tools during conversation'],['Chat voice-note permission','Voice-note capability; playback generates neural audio'],['Background photos','Separate autonomous rendering allowance'],['Camera policy','How a plausible photo is captured'],['Continuity interval','Reuse of recent photo context']],terms=['Chat media permissions','Camera policy','Self-captured','Photo continuity','Voice notes'])
add('Virtual Humans 2.0','Social profile setup and audience boundaries','''Open Edit human > Social presence. Choose an identity/profile, posting cadence, audience and the player's initial social role. These settings describe the authored social starting point; the player's private chat relationship is a related but separate history.
Cadence can be rare, weekly, daily or very active. Begin with a low cadence or automatic posts off while testing. Caption voice and posting rules should reflect the same person as Chat, but captions need not mimic private messages word for word.
Select the content categories you intend: everyday life, work, hobbies, travel, thoughts or other available categories. Photo-post ratio controls the intended balance, not proof that an image provider is ready. Adult/intimacy-related categories and intensity controls should match the authored access boundaries and supported provider route.
Audience can be public, friends/followers or private. The player's starting role can be stranger, follower, mutual, friend or a supported subscriber role. Review access rules deliberately; a new acquaintance should not accidentally start with every private-media permission.
Inspect the social drawer's Feed, Gallery and Clips after saving. Existing starter posts are authored assets. New background posts may use text requests, and newly rendered photos/clips have separate provider requirements and costs.''',shot='vh-edit-social',example='Maya: low-frequency work and hobby updates; friends/followers audience; player begins as a stranger. Captions are observant and understated. Leave automatic paid media off until one manual test succeeds.',terms=['Social audience','Posting cadence','Player social role','Content categories','Access rules'])
# Move added VH topics into the beginner-to-advanced sequence.
extras=PAGES[-3:];del PAGES[-3:]
for item,after in [(extras[0],'Routines: commitments versus activities'),(extras[1],'Photos and identity references'),(extras[2],'Social feed, gallery and media jobs')]:
 at=next(i for i,p in enumerate(PAGES) if p['title']==after);PAGES.insert(at+1,item)
for p in PAGES:
 if p['title']=='Use AI drafts without losing control':p['shot']='vh-draft-dialog'
 if p['title']=='Labs and TinyBrain local cognition':
  p['shot']='labs-panel';p['terms']+=['Shadow mode','Assist mode','Trust dashboard','Needle','Compute budget']
  p['body']='''Open Labs in the sidebar. Choose Connected local server, TinyBrain 2 - Needle, or the legacy embedded runtime. The connected route needs a running localhost server, optional key and a compatible selected model. Use Discover local servers and Test connection before saving.
TinyBrain 2 is a structured router, not a roleplay writer. Its model downloads only after Download & install. The confidence gate rejects low-confidence proposals and keeps the deterministic fallback; valid JSON does not bypass world-state validation.
Choose Off, Shadow or Assist separately for Chat, Worlds and Virtual Humans. Start in Shadow so you can inspect proposals before allowing assistance. Review the Trust dashboard for speed and validity, then enable Assist only for the workspace you tested.
Select a compute budget: Eco allows up to 6 calls/hour, Balanced up to 18, Responsive up to 40. Private diagnostics can be retained without prompt text. Save Labs setup and compare a short repeatable scene with the baseline.
A timeout or malformed result falls back to the normal engine. Your main text, image and voice connections remain separate. Labs supports selected local cognition; it cannot replace every World rule or generate the main conversation through Needle.'''
